@page "/not-found"
@layout MUI.Web.Components.Layout.MainLayout

@*
    The site's one not-found page, and the status line says so as well as the sentence.

    It is a page rather than the <NotFound> fragment inside <Router> because that fragment renders
    nothing under static server rendering — which is how a reader who mistyped a URL came to be
    handed an empty 404 with this paragraph nowhere in it. The layout is named rather than inherited
    for the same class of reason: the router applies DefaultLayout to a page it routed to, and this
    one is also rendered when nothing was routed at all, so without it the two ways of arriving here
    would hand a reader two different-looking sites.

    A slug a game used to have never reaches here — it is redirected before the route is matched
    (spec §5.7). Arriving here means no game ever answered to this URL.
*@

<SitePreview Title="Not found" Description="@PreviewCopy.Pages.NotFound" Data="false" />

<h1>Not found</h1>
<p class="dim">
    No game here. Nothing is ever deleted, so a game that once lived at this address still does —
    check the spelling.
</p>
<p class="faint"><a href="/games">Browse the directory</a></p>
